  {
    slug: 'ia',
    termEN: 'Artificial Intelligence (AI)',
    termFR: 'Intelligence artificielle (IA)',
    category: 'fundamentals',
    shortDef: "Set of techniques and systems that enable machines to perform tasks normally requiring human cognitive functions: perceiving, reasoning, deciding, learning, communicating.",
    longDef: "Artificial intelligence is a field of study born in the 1950s whose object is the design of systems capable of perceiving, reasoning, learning, deciding, or generating content. The term covers a wide diversity of techniques — from symbolic rule-based systems to modern deep neural networks, including optimization, heuristic search, and statistical methods.\n\nIn a business context, AI today mainly refers to systems based on machine learning, including deep learning and generative models. The word 'AI' is often used imprecisely in marketing — in managerial discussion, it is useful to distinguish the underlying technique (supervised ML, LLM, robotics, etc.) from the generic label.",
    example: "A Netflix recommendation system, a credit scoring model, an assistant like Claude or ChatGPT, a Universal Robots cobot, and a Stripe Radar fraud detection system are all described as 'AI' — but rely on very different techniques.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'ia-faible',
    termEN: 'Narrow AI (Weak AI)',
    termFR: 'IA faible (IA étroite)',
    category: 'fundamentals',
    shortDef: "AI specialized in a precise task (image recognition, fraud detection, language translation). It cannot transfer its skill to other domains. All AI deployed today is narrow AI.",
    longDef: "Narrow AI — also called weak AI — refers to AI systems that are currently and concretely deployed. A fraud detection model is trained specifically for fraud detection; it cannot do anything else. An LLM like GPT-4 handles a wide variety of language tasks but remains, despite its appearance of versatility, a system optimized for next-token prediction — not a general intelligent agent.\n\nThis distinction is important in managerial discussion: what exists and what is being deployed is narrow AI. Spectacular performance on certain tasks should not be interpreted as a step toward general intelligence — it remains a very advanced optimization on a defined scope.",
    example: "AlphaGo beats the best human players at Go; it cannot play chess without complete retraining. GPT-4 produces plausible texts; it has no model of the world that guarantees factual accuracy.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'ia-generale',
    termEN: 'General AI (AGI)',
    termFR: 'IA générale (AGI)',
    category: 'fundamentals',
    shortDef: "Hypothetical AI capable of performing any cognitive task a human can do, with similar or superior flexibility. Does not exist today. Subject of debates and active research.",
    longDef: "General AI — Artificial General Intelligence or AGI — refers to the hypothesis of an AI system whose cognitive capabilities would equal or surpass those of a human across the full set of intellectual tasks, and not on a narrow scope. This concept is central to public AI debates but remains speculative to date: no current system is an AGI.\n\nPredictions on the timeline of AGI arrival vary enormously between experts (from a few years to several decades, or never). For an organization, it is important to distinguish operational AI strategy — which must rely on existing narrow AI — from prospective watch on long-term evolutions. Confusing the two leads to inappropriate decisions.",
    example: "Public debate around models like GPT-4 oscillates between 'first step toward AGI' and 'advanced optimization of narrow AI'. To decide a corporate roadmap, one does not base a 12-24 month investment on the AGI hypothesis — one bases it on the demonstrated capabilities of narrow AI today.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'intelligence-collective',
    termEN: 'Collective intelligence',
    termFR: 'Intelligence collective',
    category: 'fundamentals',
    shortDef: "Human-machine system where humans and AI cooperate to produce solutions neither could produce alone. Framework popularized by Thomas Malone (MIT CCI).",
    longDef: "Collective intelligence refers to the capacity of a system composed of multiple agents — humans, machines, or a combination of both — to produce results superior to what any isolated agent could achieve. The concept is central to the work of Thomas Malone (MIT Center for Collective Intelligence) and structures part of the reflection on AI in business.\n\nIn a managerial context, collective intelligence guides organizational design: rather than replacing humans with machines, the goal is to compose hybrid teams that exploit the strengths of each type of agent. Humans bring judgment, creativity, contextualization, ethical sense; machines bring speed, memory, search across vast spaces. Designing these hybrid teams — who decides what, who supervises, who escalates — is more structuring than the choice of underlying technology.",
    example: "The Takeda × MIT CCI Superminds program applies this logic to pharmaceutical R&D: rather than an AI model alone to identify drug candidates, mixed human-machine teams are structured around complex problems in drug development.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'automatisation',
    termEN: 'Automation',
    termFR: 'Automatisation',
    category: 'fundamentals',
    shortDef: "Replacement of a human task by a deterministic machine process. Predates AI. AI extends automation to tasks requiring judgment, perception or learning.",
    longDef: "Automation is a broader term than AI. A task can be automated by fixed rules (script, programmable controller), by a classical industrial robot without AI, or by an AI system. Any deployed AI participates in automation logic, but not all automation relies on AI.\n\nIn a corporate project, decomposing a process into sub-tasks and analyzing each — automatable by rule / automatable by AI / leave to humans — is a central framing tool. The 'automate / assist / leave to human' matrix serves to arbitrate between the three options according to the nature of the task, the total cost of automation, and the associated risk.",
    example: "Accounting entry can be largely automated by rules (OCR + parsing). Fraud detection benefits from ML automation (Stripe Radar). Final validation of a mortgage loan typically remains human, with AI helping prepare the file but not deciding alone.",
