    shortDef: "ML from labeled data: each example has the expected answer. The model learns the input-output mapping. Used for fraud detection, medical diagnosis, churn prediction.",
    longDef: "Supervised learning is the most common form of ML in business. Labeled examples are available — each input is associated with the expected correct output (fraudulent transaction or not, cancer image or not, candidate to recommend or not). The model learns to generalize from these examples to predict the output on unseen new inputs.\n\nThe quality of a supervised model depends critically on the quality and representativeness of labeled data. Labeling is typically the limiting factor: it is slow, costly, and often requires expertise (radiologists for medical images, expert fraud analysts for transactions, lawyers for contractual clauses). Underestimating the labeling effort is the most common error on initial ML projects.",
    example: "The Mirai model (MIT) on breast cancer is trained on mammograms labeled with their 1-5 year clinical outcome. Stripe Radar is trained on transactions labeled legitimate or fraudulent. Both are supervised ML.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'apprentissage-non-supervise',
    termEN: 'Unsupervised learning',
    termFR: 'Apprentissage non supervisé',
    category: 'ml',
    shortDef: "ML from unlabeled data: the model discovers structures, clusters, or anomalies on its own. Used for customer segmentation, anomaly detection, exploratory analysis.",
    longDef: "Unsupervised learning works on data for which no labels are available. The objective is not to predict a known output but to discover structures in the data: natural groups (clustering), anomalies relative to the mass, hidden dimensions that effectively summarize the data.\n\nIn a business context, unsupervised learning is useful for customer segmentation (without prior assumptions about segments), anomaly detection (a transaction very different from all those known), or data compression (dimension reduction). It is generally more difficult to validate than supervised learning: without labels, how to know if the model is 'right'? Validation goes through the business utility of the discovered structures.",
    example: "A bank can use unsupervised clustering to segment its customer base, then interpret the business segments (urban young professionals, retired wealth managers, etc.). No expert provides the labels a priori — they emerge from the analysis.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'apprentissage-renforcement',
    termEN: 'Reinforcement learning',
    termFR: 'Apprentissage par renforcement',
    category: 'ml',
    shortDef: "ML where an agent learns by trial and error in an environment, receiving rewards. Used in robotics, games (AlphaGo), industrial control. Less mature in business than supervised ML.",
    longDef: "Reinforcement learning (RL) models an agent that interacts with an environment. At each action, the agent observes a new situation and receives a reward (positive or negative). Over millions of trials, the agent learns an action policy that maximizes expected cumulative reward.\n\nRL has produced spectacular results in well-defined environments like games (AlphaGo, AlphaZero, Atari). Its application in business is more delicate: precisely defining the reward function is difficult (what exactly do we want to optimize?), and the real environment is often less reproducible than a game. RL is nevertheless used in practice for robotic orchestration (Amazon DeepFleet), long-term personalization, optimization of medical treatments, and RLHF (RL from Human Feedback) used to align LLMs.",
    example: "DeepFleet, the Amazon Robotics fleet orchestration foundation model, likely uses RL components to optimize trajectories and task allocation between robots in real time. Modern LLMs (GPT-4, Claude) are fine-tuned by RLHF.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'donnees-entrainement',
    termEN: 'Training data',
    termFR: "Données d'entraînement",
    category: 'ml',
    shortDef: "Set of examples used to adjust the parameters of an ML model — their quality conditions the final performance.",
    longDef: "Training data is the raw material of supervised machine learning. Its quality, volume, representativeness, and freshness directly condition the performance of the final model. A central ML adage: 'garbage in, garbage out' — a model cannot be better than the data it has to learn from.\n\nSeveral structuring questions in any ML project: do you have enough data? Is it representative of the population on which the model will be used (selection bias, missing subgroups)? Is it up to date (potential drift)? Is labeling reliable and homogeneous? Is data governance respected (GDPR for personal data, copyright for text corpora)? These questions precede and condition any discussion on the choice of model.",
    example: "Stripe trains its fraud ML models on hundreds of millions of historical labeled transactions. The quality of labeling (legitimate/fraudulent) relies on feedback from merchants, users, and business rules — a permanent challenge.",
