  {
    slug: 'modele-generatif',
    termEN: 'Generative model',
    termFR: 'Modèle génératif',
    category: 'genai',
    shortDef: "ML model that produces new content (text, image, audio, code) by learning the distribution of training data. As opposed to a discriminative model that classifies.",
    longDef: "A generative model learns the underlying distribution of its training data and is capable of producing new plausible samples. Unlike a classifying model (which answers 'this image is a cat') or a predictive model (which answers 'this transaction has a 0.7% probability of being fraudulent'), a generative model produces new content: a text, an image, code, a synthetic voice.\n\nRecent generative models — GPT-4, Claude, Gemini for text; Midjourney, Stable Diffusion, DALL·E for images; Suno, Udio for audio — produced the public breakthrough of 2022-2024. For an organization, their business value requires rigorous framing: massive production of plausible content is not in itself a value — value emerges when the produced content integrates into a useful workflow (assistance with writing, research, programming, design).",
    example: "Morgan Stanley uses OpenAI's GPT-4 in two assistants: AI @ Morgan Stanley Assistant to retrieve information in the internal corpus, and Debrief to synthesize client meeting notes. In both cases, the model is generative (it produces text) but the scope is strictly framed.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'llm',
    termEN: 'Large Language Model (LLM)',
    termFR: 'Grand modèle de langage (LLM)',
    category: 'genai',
    shortDef: "Generative model specialized in natural language, trained on massive text corpora. Examples: GPT-4, Claude, Llama. Foundation of conversational and content generation applications.",
    longDef: "A Large Language Model (LLM) is a particular type of foundation model specialized in language. These models are trained on very large text corpora (and increasingly multimodal: text + image + audio) and learn to predict the next token in a given context. This apparently simple task, at very large scale, produces emergent complex capabilities: question understanding, reasoning on texts, code generation, translation, summary.\n\nTwo main categories: proprietary models accessible only via API (OpenAI's GPT-4, Anthropic's Claude, Google's Gemini), and open models that can be downloaded and deployed in internal infrastructure (Meta's Llama, Mistral, open source community models). The choice between the two depends on criteria such as performance, cost, data control, regulatory requirements, internal expertise.",
    example: "Morgan Stanley uses GPT-4 (proprietary LLM). GitHub Copilot relies on LLMs optimized for code. A European bank subject to strict GDPR constraints may prefer to deploy a Llama or Mistral internally to maintain control of prompts and data.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'foundation-model',
    termEN: 'Foundation model',
    termFR: 'Foundation model',
    category: 'genai',
    shortDef: "Large general-purpose model trained on a vast corpus, then specialized via fine-tuning or prompting for varied tasks. The term covers LLMs and multimodal models.",
    longDef: "The term 'foundation model' was popularized by Stanford researchers (notably Percy Liang) starting in 2021. It refers to very large models trained on massive corpora and designed to serve as a base ('foundation') for multiple downstream applications — through adaptation, fine-tuning, or simply prompting.\n\nLLMs (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini) are foundation models for text. But the concept goes beyond: foundation models are also discussed for vision (CLIP, Segment Anything), for robotics (Amazon's DeepFleet is one), for biology. The 'foundation' character lies in the generality of capabilities after pre-training, which allows economical reuse: a large model is trained once, then adapted for many tasks instead of starting from scratch each time.",
    example: "DeepFleet, the Amazon foundation model for robotic fleet orchestration announced in 2025, is explicitly positioned by Amazon as an analogue of LLMs: a large model pre-trained on massive operational data, capable of orchestrating a heterogeneous fleet in real time.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'hallucination',
    termEN: 'Hallucination',
    termFR: 'Hallucination',
    category: 'genai',
    shortDef: "Generation by an LLM of plausible but factually false content. Major risk in business deployment. Mitigated by RAG, internal sources, and human verification.",
    longDef: "A hallucination is the production by an LLM of an assertion that seems credible and well-formulated but is factually false. It can concern dates, names, numbers, citations, entirely invented but syntactically convincing bibliographic references. This phenomenon is structural: an LLM optimizes the linguistic plausibility of the next token, not factual truth.\n\nHallucinations are a central reason never to deploy an LLM alone on high-stakes decisions. Several mechanisms reduce the risk without eliminating it: RAG (anchoring answers in a curated internal corpus and requiring citations), systematic human review, explicitly limited usage scopes, training users to recognize signs of hallucination, continuous monitoring.",
    example: "Asking an LLM the list of scientific publications of a researcher can produce invented but formally credible references (plausible journal, correct citation format, coherent co-authors). It is a hallucination — that can only be detected by checking each reference.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'rag',
    termEN: 'RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)',
    termFR: 'RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)',
    category: 'genai',
    shortDef: "Architecture combining an LLM with a search engine in an external corpus — reduces hallucinations and enables traceability.",
    longDef: "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is the most widely adopted architecture for deploying LLMs in business on serious use cases. The principle: at each user query, a search engine first identifies the most relevant documents in a curated internal corpus. The key passages are then injected into the LLM context with the original query. The LLM generation is thus conditioned by internal sources.\n\nThree structural benefits: (1) **traceability** — each answer can cite source documents, which is essential in finance, law, healthcare; (2) **freshness** — corpus updates are independent of the model, no need to retrain; (3) **hallucination reduction** — the model relies on provided sources rather than generating from memory. RAG does not totally eliminate hallucinations but substantially attenuates them when the corpus is of quality.",
    example: "AI @ Morgan Stanley Assistant is a RAG system: at each advisor question, the system searches relevant documents in the internal corpus of more than 100,000 documents (research notes, reports), then asks the LLM to produce an answer grounded in these sources. The answer cites the documents used.",
