  {
    slug: 'prompt-engineering',
    termEN: 'Prompt engineering',
    termFR: 'Prompt engineering',
    category: 'genai',
    shortDef: "Practice of formulating instructions to an LLM to obtain quality answers. Discipline emerging since 2022, applicable both to individuals and to systematic workflow design.",
    longDef: "Prompt engineering is the art and practice of formulating queries (prompts) that obtain useful, precise answers from an LLM in the expected format. Several techniques have stabilized: explicit role definition, examples (few-shot), step-by-step reasoning request, specified output format (JSON, XML), length constraints, clear separation of instructions/context/data.\n\nPrompt engineering is not a purely individual competency: in business, good prompts are capitalized in internal libraries, versioned, tested, shared between teams. Prompt engineering work is progressively converging with software work — hence the appearance of dedicated tools (LangChain, LlamaIndex, prompt evaluation frameworks). However, this domain evolves rapidly: what works today may need adaptation with each new model version.",
    example: "A poorly formulated prompt for a legal assistant can produce an unusable summary. A good prompt that explicitly requests the format (sections, citations of source clauses, risk highlighting) produces an exploitable answer. The difference in quality justifies the investment in prompt engineering.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'fine-tuning',
    termEN: 'Fine-tuning',
    termFR: 'Fine-tuning',
    category: 'genai',
    shortDef: "Specialization of a pre-trained foundation model on specific data to adapt its behaviors to a domain.",
    longDef: "Fine-tuning consists of taking a pre-trained foundation model and continuing its training on a smaller but specific dataset of the target domain or task. The objective is to adapt the model's behavior to a particular context — business vocabulary, expected style, format constraints, alignment with human preferences — while benefiting from the initial investment of the foundation model.\n\nSeveral variants exist: full fine-tuning (readjusts all parameters, costly), LoRA and other efficient adaptation techniques (only adjust a fraction of parameters, much less costly), instruction tuning (training to follow instructions), RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback, alignment to preferences). The choice between fine-tuning and alternatives (RAG, advanced prompt engineering) is a classical trade-off: fine-tuning gives more control but has a cost and freezes knowledge in the model, while RAG is more flexible but does not change the default behavior of the model.",
    example: "A bank that wants an assistant adapted to its business vocabulary and internal processes can either fine-tune an open model (Llama, Mistral) or rely on RAG + prompt engineering with a proprietary model. The choice depends on the volume of data, budget, and desired control.",
