  {
    slug: 'robotique',
    termEN: 'Robotics',
    termFR: 'Robotique',
    category: 'robotics',
    shortDef: "Discipline of robots — physical machines capable of executing tasks. The current wave combines mechanics (improved sensors), software (AI for perception and decision), and economics (lower cost).",
    longDef: "Robotics combines mechanics, electronics, perception (sensors), planning, control, and increasingly AI. A major historical distinction: classical industrial robots (safety cages, repetitive tasks, high speed, heavy load) vs mobile and collaborative robotics (lighter, capable of operating near humans, more versatile).\n\nIn a business context, robotics deploys mainly in warehouse logistics (Amazon Robotics, AMR), manufacturing industry (Universal Robots, FANUC, ABB cobots), service (vacuum robots, delivery robots), and healthcare (surgical robots, assistance robots). The total cost of a robotics project is dominated not by the robot itself but by integration (sensors, tooling, business logic, safety) and change management.",
    example: "Amazon Robotics operates one million robots in its global logistics network. Universal Robots equips industrial SMEs with cobots compliant with ISO/TS 15066. Both are robotics but with very different architectures and economics.",
