  {
    slug: 'cobot',
    termEN: 'Cobot (collaborative robot)',
    termFR: 'Cobot (robot collaboratif)',
    category: 'robotics',
    shortDef: "Robot designed to operate in shared space with humans, with appropriate safety mechanisms (ISO/TS 15066). Democratization in industrial SMEs since ~2010.",
    longDef: "The cobot — collaborative robot — is a category of robots specifically designed to coexist with humans without a safety cage. The ISO/TS 15066:2016 standard defines four collaborative operation methods: safety-rated monitored stop in case of human presence, manual guiding, monitored separation, and force and power limiting. The fourth is the most used for light cobots.\n\nThe absence of a cage transforms the economics of an automation project: less dedicated space, simpler integration, possibility of using the cobot on multiple workstations by rotation. This makes robotics accessible to industrial SMEs that could not have engaged a classical robotics project. On the other hand, cobots have limited useful capacity (modest loads, average speeds) — they do not replace classical industrial robots for very high cadence or very heavy load production.",
    example: "Universal Robots is one of the world's main cobot manufacturers. Its range goes from the UR3e (3 kg load, electronics workshop) to the UR30 (30 kg, palletizing). An SME can buy a cobot, install it in a few days with a UR+ integrator, and redeploy it between several workstations as needed.",
