  // ========================================================================
  // GOVERNANCE AND SOCIETY (7)
  // ========================================================================
  {
    slug: 'biais-algorithmique',
    termEN: 'Algorithmic bias',
    termFR: 'Biais algorithmique',
    category: 'governance',
    shortDef: "Systematic distortion in an AI model's decisions, often inherited from training data. Critical in HR, credit, justice, healthcare. Mitigation via auditing and rebalancing.",
    longDef: "Algorithmic bias appears when an AI system produces systematically different results for different subgroups, without legitimate business justification. Main sources: bias in training data (under-representation of groups, biased labels, historical data reflecting past discrimination), design choices (proxy variables implicitly correlated with protected attributes), feedback loops (an initial bias reinforces itself through data generated by the system).\n\nAlgorithmic bias is one of the most mediatized risks of AI, and one of the most difficult to detect and address. Obligations are intensifying: EU AI Act requires 'data governance' including bias prevention and detection for high-risk systems, NIST AI RMF dedicates part of the Measure function to bias auditing, GDPR and anti-discrimination laws apply. Detection requires specific metrology (subgroup equity, fairness metrics) — which must be set from the design stage, not downstream.",
    example: "A CV screening model trained on historical recruitment data can learn to implicitly penalize female CVs if past practices were biased — even if gender is not an explicit input variable (the system can infer gender from other signals). The system becomes discriminatory despite a neutral initial intent.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'gouvernance-ia',
    termEN: 'AI Governance',
    termFR: 'Gouvernance IA',
    category: 'governance',
    shortDef: "Set of processes and roles structuring AI decisions in an organization: which use cases, which oversight, which controls. NIST RMF and ISO 42001 are reference frameworks.",
    longDef: "AI governance goes beyond publishing an ethics charter. It structures an operational system: internal policies, roles and responsibilities (who decides, who validates, who oversees), processes (review, audit, incident management), training, integration with existing risk management processes (cybersecurity, compliance, operational). Without this organizational dimension, principles remain ornamental.\n\nThe NIST AI Risk Management Framework proposes a structure in four interrelated functions: Govern (transverse), Map (identify context and risks), Measure (analyze and quantify), Manage (prioritize and treat). This structure is now widely adopted as a foundation, including outside the United States. It articulates with legal obligations (EU AI Act, GDPR, sectoral) that governance operationalizes.",
    example: "A corporate AI charter that says 'we respect human dignity' without specifying who validates deployments, who performs bias audits, who can suspend a system, who trains users, is not governance — it is a declaration of principles. Real governance is measured in processes, not in charters.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'ai-risk-management',
    termEN: 'AI Risk Management',
    termFR: 'AI Risk Management',
    category: 'governance',
    shortDef: "Discipline of identifying, evaluating, and mitigating risks specific to AI: technical, ethical, regulatory, reputational. Reference NIST AI RMF (4 functions: Govern, Map, Measure, Manage).",
    longDef: "AI Risk Management applies risk management disciplines well established in other domains (cybersecurity, operational, financial) to AI systems. The AI specificity lies in several factors: possible model opacity, dependence on training data, sometimes emergent behavior, rapid capability evolution, interaction with humans who do not master the underlying technology.\n\nThe NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0, published in January 2023) is the most internationally recognized operational framework. It structures the work in four functions (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage) with documented operational sub-categories. The framework is voluntary but widely adopted, including as a complement to legal obligations (notably EU AI Act). Other standards like ISO 42001 (AI Management System) or ISO 23894 (AI Risk Management) complete the ecosystem.",
    example: "An organization deploying a high-risk credit scoring system (in the AI Act sense) must conduct a systematic risk analysis: discriminatory bias risks, robustness risks against adversarial inputs, temporal drift risks, cybersecurity risks. The NIST AI RMF provides the operational method to structure this analysis.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'ai-act',
    termEN: 'EU AI Act',
    termFR: 'AI Act EU',
    category: 'governance',
    shortDef: "EU Regulation 2024/1689 on AI. Risk-based approach: prohibited / high-risk / limited / minimal. Progressive entry into application 2025-2027. Extraterritorial like GDPR.",
    longDef: "The EU AI Act — Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 — is the world's first global and structured artificial intelligence regulatory framework. Adopted in May 2024, published in the Official Journal on July 12, 2024 and entered into force on August 1, 2024, it applies progressively until August 2027 according to obligations.\n\nRisk-based approach: four categories — prohibited practices (social scoring by authorities, cognitive manipulation, mass real-time biometric identification); high-risk (recruitment, credit scoring, justice, essential public services, critical infrastructure, medical devices); limited risk (chatbots, deepfakes — transparency obligations); minimal risk (the majority of uses, no specific obligations). General-purpose models (foundation models) have a dedicated chapter. Sanctions: up to €35M or 7% of worldwide turnover. Many 'ordinary' business uses fall under high-risk — classification of each AI use case is a critical step to be conducted now.",
    example: "A CV screening system used in France falls under the high-risk category (Annex III, point 4 — employment). It must respect a dozen obligations: risk management system, data governance, technical documentation, transparency, human oversight, accuracy/robustness/cybersecurity, conformity assessment before market placement, registry in the European database.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'explicabilite',
    termEN: 'Explainability',
    termFR: 'Explicabilité',
    category: 'governance',
    shortDef: "Ability to explain how a model arrives at its decision. Required by AI Act for high-risk uses. Tension between performance (often black-box deep models) and explainability.",
    longDef: "Explainability (Explainable AI, XAI) is the capacity of an AI system to produce explanations of its decisions accessible to humans. It is required by several regulations (EU AI Act for high-risk systems, GDPR for automated decisions affecting persons), by the deontology of several professions (healthcare, finance, justice), and by the legitimate expectations of users and affected persons.\n\nTwo main approaches: intrinsically interpretable models (linear regression, simple decision trees — less powerful but readable), and post-hoc techniques that produce a posteriori explanations on complex models (LIME, SHAP, attention maps). Post-hoc techniques are an approximation and can themselves mislead — hence the importance of rigorous work on what we mean by 'explaining', to whom, with what objective. Explainability is not binary: it calibrates according to stakes.",
    example: "A credit scoring system must be able to explain why an application was refused — which is required in France by the Informatique et Libertés law and at the European level by GDPR and the AI Act. An answer like 'the model calculated 0.32' is not an explanation; the main factors must be indicated (income, history, debt ratio).",
  },
  {
    slug: 'accountability',
    termEN: 'Accountability',
    termFR: 'Accountability (responsabilité)',
    category: 'governance',
    shortDef: "Identification of who is responsible for an AI system: design, deployment, oversight, end use. AI Act distinguishes provider, deployer, importer/distributor with distinct obligations.",
    longDef: "Accountability — a term imperfectly translated as responsibility — refers to the principle whereby identifiable persons or entities are responsible for the decisions and consequences of AI systems they deploy. This principle opposes the idea that 'the algorithm decides' and that there would be no one to hold accountable for an error or damage.\n\nIn practice, accountability translates into: designation of internal roles (AI manager, ethics committee, compliance function), documentation of design and deployment decisions, traceability of automated decisions (logs, journals), incident management and remediation processes, redress channels for affected persons. It is articulated with legal requirements (EU AI Act, GDPR, common civil and criminal liability law) and internal audit practices.",
    example: "If a CV screening system wrongly rejects qualified candidates, who is responsible? The model provider? The employer who deploys it? The HR who validates? GDPR and the EU AI Act partly answer this question with shared responsibilities between provider and deployer. From the organization's side, the appointment of an AI manager and traceability of decisions are prerequisites.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'human-in-the-loop',
    termEN: 'Human-in-the-loop',
    termFR: 'Human-in-the-loop',
    category: 'governance',
    shortDef: "Architecture where a human validates or controls the AI's decisions, particularly for sensitive cases. Best practice for high-impact uses (healthcare, justice, HR).",
    longDef: "The human-in-the-loop (HITL) principle consists of maintaining a human in the decision chain of an AI system, rather than letting the system act in complete autonomy. This can take several forms: systematic validation of each decision before execution (typical in healthcare, justice, recruitment), a posteriori supervision with intervention capacity, continuous surveillance with possibility of suspension, automatic escalation to a human in complex or ambiguous cases.\n\nHITL is not just a philosophical choice. It is required by the EU AI Act for high-risk systems (Article 14 — effective human oversight), by medical deontology, by banking practices in credit matters. It is also an important operational guardrail: it allows detecting hallucinations, biases, drifts in production. The challenge is to avoid 'rubber stamping' — a human who systematically validates without truly examining.",
    example: "The Mirai model (MIT) on breast cancer produces a risk score, not a diagnosis. The radiologist integrates this score with their judgment and the patient file — they keep the clinical decision. It is an emblematic example of HITL: AI augments the radiologist rather than replacing them.",
  },

  // ========================================================================
  // METHODOLOGY (3)
  // ========================================================================
  {
    slug: 'roadmap-ia',
    termEN: 'AI roadmap',
    termFR: 'Roadmap IA',
    category: 'methodology',
    shortDef: "Structured plan of AI integration in an organization, typically 12-24 months. 5 dimensions: vision, governance, skills, infrastructure, KPIs. Capstone deliverable of the program.",
    longDef: "An AI roadmap is not a list of projects — it is a strategic document that articulates several coherent dimensions. The vision: why AI, for what business ambition, in what horizon. Priority use cases: which processes, which professions, in what order, with what expected value. Sequencing: which pilots in year 1, which scaling in year 2. Governance: who decides, who supervises, who audits, how to articulate with legal obligations (EU AI Act, sectoral). Resources: skills to recruit or train, budget, infrastructure. KPIs: how to measure real value, not just deliverables.\n\nA good roadmap balances ambition and realism. Too ambitious, it loses credibility from the first delays. Too conservative, it lets the organization fall structurally behind its competitors. The roadmap is also a living document: it is revised as the first pilots produce learnings.",
    example: "A typical AI roadmap of an average French organization over 18-24 months: T0+3 months — maturity diagnostic and identification of 3-5 candidate use cases; T0+6 months — first pilot on the most promising case (RAG on internal documentation?); T0+12 months — pilot validated or abandoned, launch of 2-3 new parallel pilots + governance structuring; T0+18-24 months — scaling of validated pilots and roadmap for the future.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'pilote-ia',
    termEN: 'AI pilot',
    termFR: 'Pilote IA',
    category: 'methodology',
    shortDef: "Limited-scope project to test the relevance and feasibility of an AI use case before generalization. Often confused with PoC: a pilot has measurable success criteria, a PoC explores.",
    longDef: "An AI pilot is a reduced-scale but representative deployment, designed to rigorously measure the real value of a use case before possible scaling. It is distinguished from the Proof of Concept (PoC) — which demonstrates technical feasibility but often in an artificial environment — by its grounding in a real context: real users, real data, real processes.\n\nA good pilot follows a structured methodology: clearly defined scope (users, processes, duration), multiple metrics (productivity, quality, satisfaction, and ideally second-order effects), comparison to a control group if possible, sufficient duration to observe lasting effects, integrated change management. The joint GitHub × Accenture study on the impact of Copilot is an emblematic example of a rigorous pilot.",
    example: "A GenAI pilot in a consulting firm could: (1) select 30 treatment consultants vs 30 control consultants, (2) measure for 3 months several dimensions (deliverable production speed, quality perceived by clients, consultant satisfaction, retention), (3) compare the two groups, (4) decide scaling / redesign / abandonment based on results.",
  },
  {
    slug: 'capstone',
    termEN: 'Capstone',
    termFR: 'Capstone (projet final intégrateur)',
    category: 'methodology',
    shortDef: "Final integrative project of a program. Here: building an AI roadmap for an organization, mobilizing the 6 modules. Defendable in C-suite or in consulting engagement.",
    longDef: "The term 'capstone' — the crowning stone of a building — refers in Anglo-Saxon pedagogical journeys to the final project that integrates all the program's concepts into a coherent production. In an executive AI training, the typical capstone is the construction of an AI roadmap for the learner's organization, mobilizing the entire journey: strategic framing (Module 1), evaluation of ML use cases (Module 2), GenAI pilot brief (Module 3), robotics opportunities if applicable (Module 4), governance and compliance (Module 5), long-term planning (Module 6).\n\nA good capstone is not a school deliverable — it is a document usable internally, defendable in the C-suite, that actually structures strategic decision. The typical format combines vision, diagnostic, priority use cases, sequencing, governance, budget, skills, KPIs, risks, compliance, and 12-24 month planning.",
    example: "For an industrial SME, a realistic capstone could be: AI roadmap prioritizing a GenAI pilot on internal technical documentation (Module 3) and a cobot pilot on one of the most arduous workstations (Module 4), with simplified but real governance (Module 5) and 18-month planning coherent with available resources.",
  },
];

const categoryLabels: Record<Term['category'], string> = {
  fundamentals: 'Fundamentals',
  ml: 'Machine Learning',
  genai: 'Generative AI',
  robotics: 'Robotics',
  governance: 'Governance and ethics',
  methodology: 'Methodology and project',
};

const categoryDescriptions: Record<Term['category'], string> = {
  fundamentals: 'Concepts framing AI in general — definition, narrow vs general, automation, collective intelligence.',
  ml: 'Machine learning — supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement, deep learning, training data.',
  genai: 'Generative AI — LLMs, foundation models, RAG, prompts, hallucinations, fine-tuning.',
  robotics: 'Industrial robotics, cobotics, autonomous mobile robots.',
  governance: 'Risk management, EU AI Act, NIST RMF, biases, accountability, explainability, human-in-the-loop.',
  methodology: 'AI project terms — pilot, roadmap, capstone.',
};

const orderedCategories: Term['category'][] = [
  'fundamentals', 'ml', 'genai', 'robotics', 'governance', 'methodology',
];

const totalTerms = terms.length;
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Glossary — ${totalTerms} essential terms`}
  description={`Glossary of ${totalTerms} AI business terms in 6 categories: fundamentals, ML, generative AI, robotics, governance, methodology. Full definitions with examples.`}
>
  <HeroSecondary
    surtitle="Glossary"
    title={`${totalTerms} essential AI terms`}
    lead={`Full definitions of ${totalTerms} terms organized in 6 categories. Each entry has a one-sentence summary, a full 2-3 paragraph definition, and a concrete business example. Click any term to expand.`}
  >
    <span slot="stats">{totalTerms} terms · 6 categories · short + full definitions · business examples</span>
  </HeroSecondary>

  <div class="ai-container ai-glossary-page">
    <article class="ai-prose">
      <section class="ai-glossary-intro">
        <p>
          This glossary brings together the {totalTerms} terms necessary to navigate the program
          comfortably. Each entry presents a <strong>one-sentence summary</strong> and unfolds
          on demand to reveal a <strong>2-3 paragraph definition</strong> with a
          <strong>concrete business example</strong>. The
          <a href={frGlossaireUrl}>French version</a> additionally contains cross-references
          between terms and module/case study links.
        </p>
      </section>

      {orderedCategories.map((cat) => {
        const list = terms.filter((t) => t.category === cat);
        return (
          <section class="ai-glossary-section" id={`category-${cat}`}>
            <header class="ai-glossary-section__head">
              <h2>{categoryLabels[cat]}</h2>
              <p>{categoryDescriptions[cat]} <span class="ai-glossary-section__count">({list.length} terms)</span></p>
            </header>
            <div class="ai-glossary-list">
              {list.map((t) => (
                <details class="ai-glossary-item" id={t.slug}>
                  <summary class="ai-glossary-item__summary">
                    <span class="ai-glossary-item__term">
                      <strong>{t.termEN}</strong>
                      {t.termEN !== t.termFR && (
                        <span class="ai-glossary-item__fr"> — <em>{t.termFR}</em></span>
                      )}
                    </span>
                    <span class="ai-glossary-item__short">{t.shortDef}</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="ai-glossary-item__details">
                    <div class="ai-glossary-item__long">
                      {t.longDef.split('\n\n').map((para) => (
                        <p>{para}</p>
                      ))}
                    </div>
                    {t.example && (
                      <aside class="ai-glossary-item__example">
                        <strong>Business example.</strong> {t.example}
                      </aside>
                    )}
                  </div>
                </details>
              ))}
            </div>
          </section>
        );
      })}

      <IndependenceNotice variant="block" />
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .ai-glossary-page { padding-block: var(--ai-space-7) var(--ai-space-8); }
  .ai-glossary-intro {
    background: var(--ai-bg-subtle);
    padding: var(--ai-space-4);
    border-radius: var(--ai-radius-md);
    border-inline-start: 4px solid var(--ai-accent-primary);
    margin-block-end: var(--ai-space-7);
  }
  .ai-glossary-intro p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ai-fg-secondary);
    line-height: var(--ai-leading-relaxed);
  }
  .ai-glossary-intro a {
    color: var(--ai-accent-primary);
    font-weight: var(--ai-weight-semibold);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .ai-glossary-intro a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  .ai-glossary-section { margin-block: var(--ai-space-7); scroll-margin-block-start: var(--ai-space-7); }
  .ai-glossary-section__head {
    margin-block-end: var(--ai-space-4);
    padding-block-end: var(--ai-space-2);
    border-block-end: 2px solid var(--ai-accent-primary);
  }
  .ai-glossary-section__head h2 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--ai-space-2);
    font-size: var(--ai-text-xl);
    color: var(--ai-fg-primary);
  }
  .ai-glossary-section__head p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ai-fg-secondary);
    line-height: var(--ai-leading-relaxed);
    font-size: var(--ai-text-sm);
  }
  .ai-glossary-section__count {
    color: var(--ai-fg-muted);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .ai-glossary-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--ai-space-3);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .ai-glossary-item {
    background: var(--ai-bg-base);
    border: 1px solid var(--ai-border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--ai-radius-sm);
    scroll-margin-block-start: var(--ai-space-7);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item[open] {
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--ai-accent-primary);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__summary {
    padding: var(--ai-space-3);
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--ai-space-1);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .ai-glossary-item__summary::marker { content: ''; }
  .ai-glossary-item__summary::before {
    content: '+';
    float: inline-end;
    font-size: var(--ai-text-lg);
    font-weight: var(--ai-weight-bold);
    color: var(--ai-accent-primary);
    margin-inline-start: var(--ai-space-2);
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .ai-glossary-item[open] .ai-glossary-item__summary::before {
    content: '−';
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__term {
    color: var(--ai-fg-primary);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__fr {
    color: var(--ai-fg-muted);
    font-size: var(--ai-text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--ai-weight-normal);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__short {
    color: var(--ai-fg-secondary);
    line-height: var(--ai-leading-relaxed);
    font-size: var(--ai-text-sm);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__details {
    padding: 0 var(--ai-space-3) var(--ai-space-3);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--ai-border-subtle);
    margin-block-start: var(--ai-space-2);
    padding-block-start: var(--ai-space-3);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__long p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--ai-space-3);
    color: var(--ai-fg-secondary);
    line-height: var(--ai-leading-relaxed);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__long p:last-child {
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__example {
    margin-block-start: var(--ai-space-3);
    padding: var(--ai-space-3);
    background: var(--ai-accent-subtle);
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--ai-accent-primary);
    border-radius: var(--ai-radius-sm);
    color: var(--ai-fg-primary);
    line-height: var(--ai-leading-relaxed);
    font-size: var(--ai-text-sm);
  }
  .ai-glossary-item__example strong {
    color: var(--ai-fg-primary);
  }
</style>
